<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">O Canada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first performed 24 June <a href="/years/1880">1880</a>; English words by Weir <a href="/years/1908">1908</a>; national anthem from 1 July <a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ô Canada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lieutenant Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, Théodore Robitaille, commissioned the song for the Saint-Jean-Baptiste Day ceremony of 1880. Calixa Lavallée composed the music, and the poet and judge Sir Adolphe-Basile Routhier then wrote French words for it, as a French Canadian patriotic song for the Saint-Jean-Baptiste Society. It was first performed on 24 June 1880 at a Saint-Jean-Baptiste Day banquet in Quebec City.</p></div>
